1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:02,505
You tap Add to cart in CloudCart.

2
00:00:03,055 --> 00:00:05,509
In under a second, many parts work together.

3
00:00:06,059 --> 00:00:08,811
Some parts run when a customer uses the shop.

4
00:00:09,361 --> 00:00:13,243
Others carry the code from a developer's laptop to that running shop.

5
00:00:13,793 --> 00:00:16,524
Today you learn all nine parts, and how they fit.

6
00:00:17,074 --> 00:00:20,018
By the end, you will explain each one in plain words.

7
00:00:22,068 --> 00:00:24,137
Split the nine parts into two groups.

8
00:00:24,687 --> 00:00:30,597
The first four run when customers use the shop: frontend, backend, API, and database.

9
00:00:31,147 --> 00:00:35,211
The other five move the code to that running shop: source control,

10
00:00:35,211 --> 00:00:35,581
build,

11
00:00:35,581 --> 00:00:36,073
release,

12
00:00:36,073 --> 00:00:36,751
deployment,

13
00:00:36,751 --> 00:00:37,675
and operations.

14
00:00:38,225 --> 00:00:42,875
DevOps work lives mostly in the second group, but you must understand the first.

15
00:00:43,425 --> 00:00:45,004
We start with the first group.

16
00:00:47,054 --> 00:00:49,934
The frontend is what the user sees and touches.

17
00:00:50,484 --> 00:00:52,937
It runs in the browser, or in a mobile app.

18
00:00:53,487 --> 00:00:57,391
In CloudCart: the product pages, the cart, and the checkout form.

19
00:00:57,941 --> 00:01:02,571
Web frontends are built with HTML, CSS, and JavaScript.

20
00:01:03,121 --> 00:01:07,217
The frontend shows information, and sends the user's actions onward.

21
00:01:07,767 --> 00:01:11,308
Never trust it to hide secrets, because users can inspect it.

22
00:01:13,358 --> 00:01:16,003
The backend is the code that runs on a server.

23
00:01:16,553 --> 00:01:18,537
It applies the rules of the business.

24
00:01:19,087 --> 00:01:22,650
For example: is the item in stock, and is the price correct?

25
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:25,760
It also decides who is allowed to do what.

26
00:01:26,310 --> 00:01:29,062
It talks to the database, and to other services.

27
00:01:29,612 --> 00:01:34,561
Backends are written in languages like Java, Python, or JavaScript on Node.

28
00:01:35,111 --> 00:01:38,695
Users never see it directly, but every action depends on it.

29
00:01:40,745 --> 00:01:44,094
How do the frontend and the backend talk? Through an API.

30
00:01:44,644 --> 00:01:51,620
API means application programming interface: a defined way for one program to ask another for something.

31
00:01:52,170 --> 00:01:57,461
Most web A P Is use H T T P requests, and return data, often as JSON.

32
00:01:58,011 --> 00:02:01,126
For example, a request to get the cart, and the reply.

33
00:02:01,676 --> 00:02:06,774
A good API is a contract: both sides agree on the requests and the responses.

34
00:02:07,324 --> 00:02:10,439
If the contract changes carelessly, the frontend breaks.

35
00:02:12,489 --> 00:02:14,814
A database stores data that must last.

36
00:02:15,364 --> 00:02:18,756
Servers can restart, but orders and accounts must remain.

37
00:02:19,306 --> 00:02:22,762
In CloudCart: products, customers, carts, and orders.

38
00:02:23,312 --> 00:02:27,643
A relational database keeps data in tables, such as PostgreSQL.

39
00:02:28,193 --> 00:02:33,164
The backend reads and writes the database. The frontend should never touch it directly.

40
00:02:33,714 --> 00:02:37,490
Data needs backups, because losing it is the costliest failure.

41
00:02:39,540 --> 00:02:40,884
Now trace one click.

42
00:02:41,434 --> 00:02:43,994
The customer taps Add to cart in the frontend.

43
00:02:44,544 --> 00:02:47,488
The frontend sends an API request to the backend.

44
00:02:48,038 --> 00:02:51,558
The backend checks stock and price, then writes to the database.

45
00:02:52,108 --> 00:02:56,865
The database confirms, the backend replies, and the frontend updates the cart.

46
00:02:57,415 --> 00:02:59,805
Four parts, one click, well under a second.

47
00:03:01,855 --> 00:03:04,735
Now the second group: how code reaches the shop.

48
00:03:05,285 --> 00:03:08,997
Source control stores every version of the code, and who changed it.

49
00:03:09,547 --> 00:03:13,771
Git is the standard tool, and GitHub and GitLab host the code online.

50
00:03:14,321 --> 00:03:18,993
It lets teams work in parallel, review changes, and go back if something breaks.

51
00:03:19,543 --> 00:03:21,804
It is the single source of truth for the code.

52
00:03:23,854 --> 00:03:26,755
A build turns source code into something that can run.

53
00:03:27,305 --> 00:03:31,593
It compiles code, downloads dependencies, and runs automated tests.

54
00:03:32,143 --> 00:03:36,602
The result is called an artifact, such as a package or a container image.

55
00:03:37,152 --> 00:03:40,821
A build should be repeatable: the same code gives the same result.

56
00:03:41,371 --> 00:03:45,766
Tools include Maven, for Java, and Docker, for container images.

57
00:03:47,816 --> 00:03:51,507
A release is a specific, versioned build, approved to go live.

58
00:03:52,057 --> 00:03:55,449
For example, CloudCart version one point four point two.

59
00:03:55,999 --> 00:04:00,223
Deployment is the act of putting that version onto a running environment.

60
00:04:00,773 --> 00:04:06,213
A deployment can go to a test environment, or to production, which a later lesson covers.

61
00:04:06,763 --> 00:04:11,285
Release and deployment are related, but teams use the words a little differently.

62
00:04:11,835 --> 00:04:13,755
So always ask what a team means.

63
00:04:15,805 --> 00:04:18,365
Operations keeps the running system healthy.

64
00:04:18,915 --> 00:04:21,688
It watches performance, errors, and availability.

65
00:04:22,238 --> 00:04:25,865
It responds when something fails, and applies updates and fixes.

66
00:04:26,415 --> 00:04:30,127
Monitoring shows problems, often before customers report them.

67
00:04:30,677 --> 00:04:34,901
In DevOps, developers and operations share this responsibility.

68
00:04:36,951 --> 00:04:39,362
Here is every part, mapped to CloudCart.

69
00:04:39,912 --> 00:04:42,301
Frontend: the product and cart pages.

70
00:04:42,851 --> 00:04:45,518
Backend: the stock, price, and order rules.

71
00:04:46,068 --> 00:04:48,180
API: the requests between them.

72
00:04:48,730 --> 00:04:50,948
Database: the products and the orders.

73
00:04:51,498 --> 00:04:53,589
Source control: the code, in Git.

74
00:04:54,139 --> 00:04:56,336
Build: package the code into an image.

75
00:04:56,886 --> 00:04:59,276
Release: version one point four point two.

76
00:04:59,826 --> 00:05:01,852
Deployment: put it on the servers.

77
00:05:02,402 --> 00:05:05,026
Operations: watch it, and keep it healthy.

78
00:05:07,076 --> 00:05:08,932
Watch out for four common myths.

79
00:05:09,482 --> 00:05:11,931
Myth one: the database is the backend.

80
00:05:11,931 --> 00:05:12,447
In fact,

81
00:05:12,447 --> 00:05:13,736
the backend is code,

82
00:05:13,736 --> 00:05:15,605
and the database stores data.

83
00:05:16,155 --> 00:05:21,765
Myth two: an API is only for outside developers. Your own frontend uses one too.

84
00:05:22,315 --> 00:05:26,016
Myth three: release and deployment are always the same thing.

85
00:05:26,016 --> 00:05:27,047
They are related,

86
00:05:27,047 --> 00:05:28,139
but not identical.

87
00:05:28,689 --> 00:05:32,042
Myth four: operations begins after the project ends.

88
00:05:32,042 --> 00:05:34,492
It begins when the first version runs.

89
00:05:35,042 --> 00:05:38,413
Knowing the right words prevents expensive misunderstandings.

90
00:05:40,463 --> 00:05:41,338
Quick check.

91
00:05:41,888 --> 00:05:44,405
What is the difference between frontend and backend?

92
00:05:44,955 --> 00:05:46,256
What does an API do?

93
00:05:46,806 --> 00:05:48,918
Name the five delivery parts, in order.

94
00:05:49,468 --> 00:05:52,199
What is the difference between a release and a deployment?

95
00:05:52,749 --> 00:05:56,802
Objective covered: MC01-O04.

96
00:05:57,352 --> 00:05:59,422
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

97
00:06:05,972 --> 00:06:07,700
Next lesson: who does what.

98
00:06:08,250 --> 00:06:10,127
